Dialogue: 0,0:00:00.00,0:00:09.00,Default,,0,0,0,,Satellite hobbyists need to know when to look, where to point, and how the radio frequency shifts.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:09.00,0:00:18.50,Default,,0,0,0,,zombieSatellite brings those steps together on a phone, giving forgotten spacecraft a new role in hands-on learning.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:18.50,0:00:26.50,Default,,0,0,0,,The archive covers seven zombie satellites, with documented stories and sources. Five support playable sessions.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:26.50,0:00:33.50,Default,,0,0,0,,For example, radio amateur Scott Tilley rediscovered the IMAGE spacecraft in twenty eighteen.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:33.50,0:00:40.50,Default,,0,0,0,,The augmented reality view places the calculated satellite direction over the phone camera.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:40.50,0:00:49.50,Default,,0,0,0,,Switch spacecraft to explore their different positions. The camera is a visual backdrop, not a radio receiver.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:49.50,0:00:56.50,Default,,0,0,0,,Without the camera, the sky view still shows orbit geometry and direction cues.
Dialogue: 0,0:00:56.50,0:01:05.00,Default,,0,0,0,,These cues help you orient the phone, including when the selected satellite is outside the view.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:05.00,0:01:15.00,Default,,0,0,0,,Public orbital data and the observer location determine the next predicted pass. Times are shown in coordinated\Nuniversal time.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:15.00,0:01:24.00,Default,,0,0,0,,Choose a satellite, inspect the pass, and open its session. Observer coordinates are hidden here for privacy.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:24.00,0:01:32.00,Default,,0,0,0,,This demonstration plays a predicted pass at sixty times normal speed, with automatic pointing enabled.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:32.00,0:01:41.50,Default,,0,0,0,,During the pass, the lock indicator shows pointing accuracy, while the Doppler readout tracks the computed frequency\Nshift.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:41.50,0:01:51.00,Default,,0,0,0,,The beacon sound is synthesized. The app receives no satellite radio signal. Real reception requires your own radio.
Dialogue: 0,0:01:51.00,0:02:01.00,Default,,0,0,0,,At the end of the pass, review locked time, lock ratio, and pointing error, then open the card collection.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:01.00,0:02:09.50,Default,,0,0,0,,Each completed session produces a Q S L card containing the satellite, timing, and session details.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:09.50,0:02:18.50,Default,,0,0,0,,Every card identifies the session as simulated, so it cannot be mistaken for proof of radio reception.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:18.50,0:02:26.00,Default,,0,0,0,,The project was built with Kiro, using eleven feature specifications and two steering files.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:26.00,0:02:34.00,Default,,0,0,0,,Requirements, design, and tasks structure the work. Steering documents define project scope and development rules.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:34.00,0:02:42.50,Default,,0,0,0,,This evidence is included in the repository, covering orbit prediction, pointing, sessions, and the app shell.
Dialogue: 0,0:02:42.50,0:02:53.00,Default,,0,0,0,,zombieSatellite makes the first steps of satellite tracking accessible on a phone. The orbit is real. The beacon is\Nsimulated.